DP  - 2018
TI  - Application of 3D Printing for Patient-Specific Silicone Stents: 1-Year Follow-Up 
      on 2 Patients.
PG  - 488-494
LID - 10.1159/000489669 [doi]
AB  - Managing complex benign airway disease is a major challenge in interventional 
      pulmonology. Airway stent placement can result in complications due to a variety 
      of factors, including poor fit of the stent in the airway. We report the 1-year 
      outcome of 2 patients with airway disease caused by granulomatosis with 
      polyangiitis (Wegener's) affecting the left main bronchus and secondary carina. 
      These patients had not responded to systemic therapy or standard bronchoscopic 
      techniques and had complications with commercially available airway stents. We 
      describe a first-in-human, clinical experience to address these issues. Using 
      computed tomography (CT) imaging and 3D printing technology, we generated and 
      implanted patient-specific silicone airway stents to address airway disease. 3D 
      patient-specific stent prescription was created using a CT scan of the patient's 
      chest and a proprietary software package originally developed for orthopedic 
      surgical planning. Silicone stents were manufactured and implanted. Observation 
      for > 1 year after implantation compared to the 6 months prior to 
      patient-specific stent implant were compared for the number, clinically required 
      stent changes, procedure time, and general clinical improvement per usual 
      standard of care after airway stenting. Patients showed improved durability, a 
      shorter procedure time, and improvement of patient-reported symptoms leading to a 
      reduced need for stent changes and modifications. The use of 3D printing 
      technology to make patient-specific silicone stents is feasible with early 
      clinical proof of concept noting a durable improvement over 1 year of follow-up.
