DP  - 2026 Mar
TI  - Staged Airway Stenting for Life-Threatening Tracheogastric Fistula (TGF) After 
      Esophagectomy: A Case Report.
PG  - e72307
LID - 10.1002/ccr3.72307 [doi]
LID - e72307
AB  - Tracheogastric fistula (TGF) is a rare but lethal complication following 
      esophagectomy. Management of critical TGF cases with hemorrhage and respiratory 
      failure remains challenging due to limited therapeutic options. We report a case 
      of a 60-year-old man who developed life-threatening TGF with massive bleeding 
      from the cervical anastomotic, airway, and thoracic drain sites 11 days 
      postesophagectomy. Emergency bedside gastroscopy and bronchoscopy confirmed a 
      left bronchial TGF. A staged intervention protocol was implemented: (1) Y-shaped 
      airway stent placement during mechanical ventilation for hemorrhage control; (2) 
      custom-made extended-length airway stent for precise fistula sealing; (3) 
      combined negative-pressure drainage, targeted antibiotics, and nutritional 
      support. After 36 days, complete fistula healing was achieved with stent removal. 
      This case demonstrates that staged airway stenting with multimodal intensive care 
      unit (ICU) support can successfully manage critical postesophagectomy TGF, 
      providing a potential therapeutic paradigm for this devastating complication.
